# OpenLot Code Generation Guidelines

## 0. Context you must hold in mind

OpenLot is a decentralized, self-hosted, open-source alternative to Roblox. The stack is **Godot 4 with C#**, used as an infrastructure layer only: Godot's renderer, physics, networking and gizmo systems are borrowed, but the animation system, UI system, and modding/plugin architecture are custom-built on top rather than using Godot's built-in equivalents for those four. Scripting exposed to end users runs through a **Lua layer** (via an xLua-derived binding) sitting on top of the C# core — most gameplay/creation-side logic that a *creator* writes will be Lua calling into a defined C# API surface, not raw C# in the lot itself.

Two audiences work on code here: Rolkka (the developer) and AI tools generating C#/Lua on his behalf, usually from an organized task description he writes and expects translated into working code. Because Rolkka can't always live-verify unfamiliar generated code against a black box, **code must be predictable, inspectable, and consistent with existing patterns** — favor clarity and traceability over cleverness every time.

---

## 1. Architecture Rules

### 1.1 Layering — respect it strictly

```

Lua (creator-facing scripts)
↓ calls into
C# API surface (LuaManager-equivalent, explicit bound methods)
↓ built on
Godot C# layer (rendering, physics, scene tree, GLTF, gizmos, Networking)

```
- Never let Lua touch Godot APIs directly. All Lua access must go through the defined C# API surface — this is the security/sandboxing boundary and the plugin-tier boundary (see §1.4). If a new capability is needed in Lua, add a bound method to the API surface; don't punch a hole through it.
- Custom systems (animation `.lanim`, UI, modding) should treat Godot as a service they consume, not a framework they're embedded inside. Avoid writing code that only works if it lives inside Godot's own editor/scene-authoring workflow — OpenLot's tooling is meant to be usable independent of the Godot editor at runtime.
- Don't reach "up" a layer. Godot-layer code must never call into Lua or the plugin system directly; it exposes hooks/events that upper layers subscribe to.

### 1.2 Custom systems stay custom
- Animation: use the `.lanim` format and OpenLot's own Animator/playback pipeline. Do not wire gameplay code directly to Godot's `AnimationPlayer`/`AnimationTree` as the source of truth — those are implementation details the custom system may use internally, not the public contract.
- UI: use OpenLot's own UI system (ImGui-derived heritage from the terminal work) for anything player/creator-facing. Don't introduce Godot's Control-node UI as a second UI paradigm inside gameplay-facing surfaces — it's fine only where it's purely an internal editor/debug convenience, and must be clearly marked as such.
- When in doubt whether something is "Godot infrastructure" (fine to use directly) or "OpenLot custom system territory" (must go through the custom layer), default to treating it as custom-system territory and ask/flag rather than assume.

### 1.3 Networking and state.
- The core exposes only narrow enforcement primitives (kick/ban/query a peer) to plugins. Moderation *policy*, commands and workflows are plugin territory (see §1.4) and are never baked into the core. Whoever implements them: enforcement must be host-authoritative, and must never trust client-reported state for enforcement decisions.

### 1.4 Plugin/modding tiers — don't blur them
- **Tier 1 (Terminal apps/ gameplay client plugins):** sandboxed, terminal/window-API-only. Never give a Tier 1 plugin direct access to scene/object state.
- **Tier 2 (Creation-environment plugins):** access build-mode's defined plugin API (tools/panels/importers) only. Never let a Tier 2 plugin reach into rendering/physics internals directly.
- **Tier 3 (Deep/engine-level):** the only tier allowed to touch Godot internals (shaders, custom optimizations). Code written at this tier must be clearly separated and documented as Tier 3 — never write Tier 3-reaching code and expose it as if it were a Tier 1/2 capability.
- When generating a plugin or plugin-facing API, always state which tier it targets and justify why it needs that tier's access level.

---

## 2. Style Rules

### 2.1 C# conventions
- Follow standard .NET naming: `PascalCase` for classes/methods/public members, `camelCase` for locals/private fields, `_camelCase` or `camelCase` with a leading underscore for private backing fields — pick one and stay consistent within a file/module (match whatever the surrounding file already uses; don't introduce a third convention into an existing file).
- Explicit types over `var` when the inferred type isn't obvious at a glance from the right-hand side — readability for a solo dev re-reading AI-generated code later matters more than brevity.
- Singletons (e.g. `LuaManager`-style) should be explicit and minimal — a clear static accessor, not a hidden service-locator pattern layered on top.

### 2.2 Lua-facing API design
- Every method bound from C# into Lua should have an unambiguous, verb-first name (`SetPosition`, `SpawnObject`, not `Position` as a setter-getter overload) — Lua callers need to be able to tell intent from the name alone.
- Keep the bound API surface minimal and additive. Don't expose broad/general-purpose C# objects into Lua "for convenience" — expose specific, narrow methods, so the sandboxing boundary in §1.1 stays enforceable.
- Document every newly bound method with what it does, its parameters, and any side effects, right next to the binding — this is the contract creators will actually script against.

### 2.3 Comments and structure
- Comment *why*, not *what*, for anything non-obvious — especially around any borrowed/adapted third-party pattern (e.g. ImGuizmo integration, GLTF loading quirks) so Rolkka can tell later what's OpenLot-original vs. adapted.
- Keep functions short and single-purpose; a function that spans a full screen without a clear single responsibility should be split, especially in AI-generated code where verification-by-reading is the main review tool available.
- No dead code, no commented-out old implementations left in place — if it's superseded, remove it; Git history is the record, not inline comments.

### 2.4 Performance and bloat discipline
- Avoid per-frame allocations in hot paths (`Update`/render loop equivalents) — reuse buffers/objects rather than allocating new ones every frame, given the project's explicit goal of staying lightweight versus Unity/Roblox bloat.
- Don't add a dependency (NuGet package, external library) for something a small amount of original code can do — every added dependency works against the project's "avoid bloat, avoid external engine lock-in" thesis. If a library genuinely saves real effort on a hard problem (e.g. ImGuizmo for gizmo math), that's a deliberate, named exception — not a default.
- Prefer editing/updating existing state over recreating objects, especially for anything touching the scene graph or Lua-bound objects, since these can be called frequently from creator scripts.

---

## 3. What NOT to do

- Don't silently change public API method signatures that Lua scripts or plugins already depend on — that's a breaking change to creator-facing contracts and must be called out explicitly, not slipped in.
- Don't add speculative "just in case" configurability or abstraction layers beyond what the current task needs — this project's stated philosophy is lean and purpose-built, not generalized-for-hypothetical-futures.
- Don't write code that only makes sense inside the Godot editor's authoring flow when the target is runtime/build-mode behavior — OpenLot's build mode is its own authored experience, not a thin wrapper around the Godot editor.
- Don't invent new naming/structural conventions when an existing pattern in the codebase already covers the case — match what's there first, propose a change separately if the existing pattern seems wrong.

---

# OpenLot Optimization & Memory-Safety Review Prompt

You are reviewing OpenLot's codebase (Godot 4 + C#, with a Lua scripting layer bound through a C# API surface) for **memory leaks, unnecessary bloat, and performance issues only**. You are not reviewing naming conventions, architecture layering, or style — assume those are handled elsewhere. Stay scoped to runtime cost and memory safety.

## Hard constraints — do not violate these while optimizing

1. **Never change visual/UI style or behavior.** No adjusting spacing, colors, layout, animation feel, or any user-visible output as a side effect of an optimization. If a perf fix would risk a visible change, flag it instead of applying it.
2. **Never cut or reduce a feature.** Do not remove functionality to "simplify" something for performance. If a feature is the actual cause of a serious perf problem, flag it with an explanation — don't silently strip it.
3. **Preserve all existing public API/Lua-bound method signatures.** Optimizing internals is fine; changing what a creator's Lua script can call, or what parameters it takes, is not — that's a breaking change outside this task's scope.

## What to check for

### Memory leaks
- Event/signal subscriptions that are never unsubscribed (Godot signal connections, C# events) — especially on objects that get destroyed/freed while still subscribed elsewhere
- Godot node references held after the node is freed (`QueueFree()`'d nodes still referenced elsewhere)
- Lua-side objects or closures holding references to C# objects longer than needed, preventing GC
- Static collections (lists, dictionaries, caches) that grow unbounded — added to but never pruned or cleared
- Disposable resources (textures, meshes, file handles, network connections) not explicitly released when no longer needed

### Unnecessary bloat
- Per-frame allocations in hot paths (`_Process`/`_PhysicsProcess` equivalents, render loop, anything called every tick) — flag allocations that could be hoisted out or reused via pooling
- Redundant work repeated every frame that could be cached or computed once (e.g. recomputing something static, re-fetching a reference that doesn't change)
- Unused imports, dead fields, or unreferenced private methods left behind from earlier iterations
- Overly broad object graphs passed around when only a small piece of data is actually needed (e.g. passing a whole GameObject-equivalent when only a position is used)
- Boxing/unboxing in hot paths (value types being implicitly boxed into object references repeatedly)

### Performance
- O(n²) or worse patterns where a better data structure (dictionary lookup vs. linear scan, etc.) would avoid it, especially in code that runs per-object or per-frame across many objects (character updates, physics/collision checks, Lua API calls)
- Synchronous work on the main thread that blocks rendering/input (file I/O, network calls) where it could be async/deferred
- Physics/collision checks running more often or on more objects than necessary (e.g. no broad-phase culling before narrow-phase checks)
- String concatenation/formatting in hot paths where a non-allocating alternative exists
- Lua↔C# boundary crossings happening more often than needed in a tight loop, where batching the calls would reduce marshaling overhead

---

# OpenLot Roadmap

**Target MLP/MVP:** late November 2026 (Godot-based alpha/experimental stage)
**Target full release:** March 2027
**Stack:** Godot 4 (C#) as infrastructure layer (rendering, physics, gizmos) — custom animation system, custom UI system, custom modding/plugin architecture built on top

Legend for difficulty tags carried over from the original planning pass:
🔴 Red = extremely hard / needs research · 🟠 Orange = Rolkka organizes, AI translates to C# · 🟡 Yellow = repetitive, AI handles smoothly · 🟩 Green = only Rolkka, fine-tuning/feel
Status tags, separate from difficulty ratings: ⛔️ Blocked = waits on another milestone (named inline) · ⭕️ Needs expansion = the milestone is under-specified and wants a detail pass before it can be built

