> audio all read/write the `.lot` archive. The sections that follow keep their relative order (§5 Terminal, §6
> Characters, §7 Animation, §8 Networking), so this block is the only one out of numeric sequence. §3 resumes
> at §3.5 just below.

## 4. Save/load & lot persistence — The `.lot` Archive *(Phase 1 — see Roadmap Execution Stages)*

### Milestone 4.1 — `.lot` archive format, `LotVFS` & versioning
**Implementation status:** the write half is complete; the read-into-the-scene half is §4.2. Landed: `builder/LotArchive.cs` owns the format (manifest / node-record / property-bag / environment records, their JSON encoding via Godot's `Json`, and the version gate); `builder/LotArchiveIo.cs` is the single zip writer plus the manifest+payload reader; `builder/LotVfs.cs` is the streaming read path (`Open` / `HasEntry` / `ReadEntry` / `ReadEntryText` — the roadmap's `OpenLot` / `TryReadAsset` naming was not used, the narrower verbs are the contract); `builder/LotSceneWalk.cs` captures the live `LotRoot`/`LotUIRoot` into a `LotDocument`; `builder/LotArchiveCollect.cs` gathers script text and texture bytes into the archive's `scripts/` + `assets/` (+ their `Secret_Code/` + `Secret_Asset/` counterparts); `builder/LotSave.cs` is the one pipeline manual save and autosave both call; `builder/LotAutosave.cs` is the periodic snapshot, the crash marker and the recovery prompt; `builder/SecretContent.cs` is the editor-side hide/reveal gate. Verified headless by `LotArchiveSelfTest` (**175 checks, 0 failures**): the version gate both ways; the manifest / node-record / environment JSON round-trip through the real encoder; the VFS read path; a captured scene surviving the round-trip; script + asset routing (base content to the secret folders, creator content to the normal ones); the full save pipeline from live nodes to a re-readable archive; autosave's guard rails plus the marker contract; the secret-content gate (default hidden, only base content affected, reveal/hide, and the caution's wording); and the section's own collection walk (`HierarchyPanel.CollectSecretRoots`), which is what proves a revealed section finds the base script instead of drawing an empty heading. The secret rows' on-screen layout is a rendering outcome checked by eye, the same split the project uses for the wallpaper.
Three Godot facts the round-trip pinned down, because each is easy to get wrong: `Json.Stringify` writes a `Vector3`/`Color` as the **string** `"(1, 2, 3)"` — not an object, and *not* the Variant text form `GD.StrToVar` accepts, so `ReadVector3` / `ReadColor` parse that tuple themselves; and `ZipReader.GetFiles()` lists **directory entries** (`scripts/`) as well as files, so `LotVfs.Entries` filters names ending in `/` and means "entries you can actually read".
**Still open in this milestone:** (a) restoring a `LotDocument` **into** the live scene — deliberately deferred to §4.2, so nothing here half-loads; (b) no migration exists because v1 is the first version. `LotSave.Write` and the collection pass are proven; wiring them to the File menu is §4.2.
- [x] Define the `.lot` as a **single-file Zip archive bundle** — one portable lot document, not a loose folder tree. Fixed entries:
  - `manifest.json` — **format version**, lot metadata (name, author, created/modified), **`maxPlayers`**, and the **entry script** a headless host boots (§8.1), so the archive declares its own server entry point instead of a hardcoded path.
  - `lot.json` — the scene payload: scene hierarchy, per-object transforms, `LotUIRoot` elements (§5.3), **environment settings** (`LotEnvironmentSettings`, unblocks §2.5), and the `LotPropertyRegistry` **bindings** (the 2.3 table is the declaration; this is the stored value set per object).
  - `scripts/` — the local entity + root Lua scripts: the `.lot`-local home for what is `user://Scripts/*.lua` today (`ScriptManager.ScriptsDir`, `BuilderScene.EnsureDefaultLotScript`). Script nodes reference entries here by archive-relative path, so a lot is self-contained and portable.
  - `assets/` — embedded binaries: 3D models (`.glb`), textures and audio. These are the bytes §6.2 (GLTF), the 2.3 Texture property and §7.2 (audio) load at runtime, instead of files that exist only on the author's machine.
  - **`Secret_Code/` + `Secret_Asset/`** — the base-content counterparts of the two folders above, holding what a creator does not normally edit (base character code, base character formats, and later other base material). They are the **same format** as the normal folders — same writer, same reader, same VFS path — and the only difference is derived from the path prefix (`LotArchive.IsSecretPath`), so no per-entry flag was added to the v1 record. Routing rule, stated once in `LotArchiveCollect`: the script a new lot ships with (`BuilderScene.DefaultLotScriptName`) goes to `Secret_Code/`, a texture on a Capsule (the base character) goes to `Secret_Asset/`, everything else to the normal folders.
  - **Base content is separated in the editor, not just marked** (follow-up fix). The first cut filtered the base script row in place, which left it listed *inline among the creator's own nodes* — the one arrangement where a shared, proprietary file gets edited by muscle memory. Now: `builder/SecretContent.cs` owns the visibility decision and nothing else, `HierarchyPanel.DrawNode` **always** excludes base content from the main tree (not only while hidden), and `DrawSecretSection` draws it below the normal tree under its own heading — an amber `Secret Content (proprietary)` title, a caution line, a separator, and a `[base]` marker on each row. The section is drawn before the marquee so its rows still take part in marquee hit-testing, and it renders through the same row code, so selecting, renaming, dragging and the context menu behave identically once revealed. Selection is a **second** exposure, so `InspectorPanel.DrawHeader` prints the same heading + caution before any control when the selected node is base content.
  - **`IsSecretContent(node)` is the section-membership test and never changes with the reveal state** — `Revealed` only decides whether the section is drawn at all. A classification that flipped with the toggle is precisely how base content would end up inline again, so a self-test pins it across both states.
  - Hiding again needs no confirmation; revealing is **View → "Show Secret Content…"**, which opens a caution modal first ("universal and proprietary to OpenLot … ONLY proceed if you know what you are doing"). Visibility only: a hidden node stays in the scene, still runs and still saves — and the state is session-only, so a restart is hidden again. **`Secret_Asset/` content is not surfaced anywhere yet** because the editor has no archive browser to list it in; that lands with §14.6.
  - Known cosmetic quirk: a drag dropped near the secret section draws its insertion line inside that section, but the landing is resolved against the node's *real* parent (`LotRoot`) because the section is a view, not a container — so a drop can never move something *into* secret content. Not fixed, deliberately: fixing it means teaching the drop feedback about the section for no behavioural gain.
- [x] Introduce **`LotVFS`** — a Virtual File System that **streams assets directly out of an open `.lot` archive without extracting them to disk**. Primitives: Godot's `ZipReader` (`Open` → `FileExists` / `GetFiles` / `ReadFile`) to read and `ZipPacker` to write; the archive index is read once and entry bytes are handed straight to Godot's **buffer** loaders — `Image` `Load*FromBuffer` for textures (the path 2.3's `LotTextureCache` already uses), `GltfDocument.AppendFromBuffer` + `GltfState` for models (§6.2), and the matching buffer path for audio (§7.2). `LotVFS` is the **single read path** for every asset consumer, so there is one loader and one disposal rule. Tier note: this is **Godot-infrastructure-facing** code — it owns file/zip/buffer access and exposes only narrow, verb-first accessors (`OpenLot`, `ReadEntry`, `TryReadAsset`) upward; Lua never touches it directly (§1.1).
- [x] Versioning + migration: a `formatVersion` in `manifest.json`; an older file is either migrated or rejected with a clear, readable error — never a silent half-load. Document the migration step for every bump.
- [x] **Autosave and crash recovery** (moved here from §2.4 — there was nothing to serialize until this format existed): a periodic autosave to **`user://autosave.lot`**, a crash marker written on unclean exit, and a recovery prompt on boot that offers the recovery file over the last manual save. Autosave rides the same writer as a manual save (§4.2), so it can never emit a file the loader rejects. **The prompt now actually loads the snapshot** (it used to only keep the file and point at the File menu, which was true while §4.2 was unbuilt): `Recover` runs the same `LotLoad` path Open Lot… does, but **does not adopt the snapshot's path as the lot's file** — a recovered lot has no destination the creator chose, so it is marked dirty (quitting warns, `Ctrl+S` asks for a file) and the snapshot is deleted **only after a save that actually succeeded** (`Builder.WriteLot`), so a failed or cancelled save can never destroy the only copy. The load is deferred to the top of the next layout pass rather than run where the modal is drawn, because the modal renders *after* the panels have walked the scene and replacing the lot there would free nodes they had just read.
**Window close is routed through the unsaved-changes prompt (follow-up fix).** Clicking the OS window's X used to let Godot quit immediately, which bypassed the unsaved-changes prompt *and* — because the scene tree is still torn down — ran `_ExitTree`, cleared the crash marker and so made an abandoned session look "clean", suppressing the recovery prompt as well. `Builder` now sets `GetTree().AutoAcceptQuit = false` in `_Ready`, handles `NOTIFICATION_WM_CLOSE_REQUEST` (ignored while the unsaved-changes prompt is already up, so a second X click cannot discard the work being asked about), and hands `AutoAcceptQuit` back in `_ExitTree` so the terminal keeps Godot's default. X therefore means "close OpenLot" (`RequestQuit(true)` → `QuitApplication`), not "go back to the main menu", which is what the menu's Exit does. Two related data-loss bugs in the same path were fixed with it: the prompt's **"Save & Quit" only cleared the dirty flag and never wrote a file** (a 2.3-era stub) — it now saves through the real pipeline, parking the quit intent in `_quitAfterSave` so a save that still needs a destination from the file dialog can finish first, and a **failed** save cancels the quit rather than ending the session on top of it; and the pause menu's "Save Lot" button, which still printed "persistence lands with milestone 2.3", now calls the real save.
**Acceptance criteria:** The `.lot` format is documented and versioned, a file written by an older build is either migrated or rejected with a clear error rather than failing silently, a lot is recoverable after a crash or restart without losing work, and an asset loads from inside the archive with no extracted copy left on disk.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 zip bundle + manifest split · 🟠 `LotVFS` buffer streaming · 🟠 versioning/migration · 🟠 autosave/crash recovery

### Milestone 4.2 — Save/load
**Implementation status:** landed and verified headless. `builder/LotSceneLoad.cs` is the mirror of `LotSave` — it restores a decoded archive into a lot's two containers in four ordered phases (nodes → lot UI → asset bytes → properties), and the ordering is load-bearing because a part's Texture property only accepts an id the cache already knows. `builder/LotLoad.cs` is the single load pipeline and is **two-phase**: it decodes and validates the whole archive *before* touching the scene, so a newer-than-this-build file, a missing payload or a corrupt zip is reported and the creator's current work is left exactly as it was. `BuilderScene.ReplaceLotFromArchive` owns the scene-level consequences (stop the runtime → drop selection and history → empty both containers → restore → apply environment → reset and re-issue handles → restart the runtime), and `BuilderScene.EmptyContainer` frees immediately rather than queueing, because a queued free would leave the old nodes in the tree for the rest of the frame and the new ones would be added alongside them. `builder/LotFilePicker.cs` is the `.lot` browser (Save As / Open), reused for both actions and justified the same way as `ImageFilePicker`. The File menu's Save Lot / Save Lot As… / Open Lot… are live (`Ctrl+S` too), disabled inside a player session, and report their outcome — including anything that could not be embedded — in a message modal. `LotAutosave` now writes through `LotSave.WriteScene`, the same scene-level pipeline a manual save uses.
Two additions this milestone needed, both stated as such rather than slipped in: (a) **`LotTextureCache.ImportFromBuffer`** registers archived bytes under the id the record already references. Without it a loaded texture would dangle, because `MakeAssetId` hashes the *source path* — which does not exist on another machine — so a save→load→save round-trip only stays stable if the id is preserved. Its decode step is the same sniff-and-dispatch path `ImportFromFile` uses (`DecodeBytes`), so there is still one image loader. (b) **Scripts are now recorded** in `lot.json` as a `"script"` record kind carrying only the file *name*. Inferring them from the archive's `scripts/` + `Secret_Code/` entries would have flattened every script onto the lot root, and the context menu's "Insert Script" attaches a script to any entity — so a root-only restore would silently change what the lot does. This is an additive change to v1 (a new record kind, no changed field) made before any lot shipped, so no migration is needed; the file name is stored rather than a path because a machine-local path must never enter a lot.
Verified by `LotArchiveSelfTest` (**225 checks, 0 failures**), including the full acceptance path: a live lot with two parts, an attached texture, a root script *and* a script attached to a part, plus a UI element, is saved, every reference released, and loaded into fresh containers — asserting structure (the attached script is still under its part), transforms, properties, UI rect/visibility, the recovered image (same id, really decoded from the archive, same pixels), and the manifest's name and creation stamp. Also covered: the kind-tag round trip both directions, `ImportFromBuffer` (including a repeat import reusing the entry, and a non-image being refused), and the unknown-kind path.
**Known gaps, stated rather than implied:** `LotSceneLoad.Restore` is what the tests drive; the thin scene-level glue in `BuilderScene.ReplaceLotFromArchive` (selection/history drop, container emptying, handle reset, runtime restart) is not covered headlessly, because it replaces the one live lot the other suites depend on — it is compile-verified and checked by eye in the client, like the menu/dialog flow. Cross-device transfer is a plain file copy and was not exercised on a second machine here. Writing a loaded lot's scripts back to `user://Scripts/` means **the archive wins** over a stale local copy of the same file name (deliberate — a lot ships its own code).
- [x] Save a creation as a single `.lot` file (the writer §4.1-autosave also uses).
- [x] Load a saved creation, same device — read through `LotVFS`, restoring scene, `LotUIRoot`, environment and assets in one pass.
- [ ] Transfer and load a saved creation on a different device (may be held for later) — the archive is self-contained, so this is a file copy rather than a folder-tree move.
**Acceptance criteria:** A lot built on one machine can be saved, copied to a second machine, and loaded there with all objects, UI elements, scripts and embedded assets intact.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

**What this section unblocks / re-indexes**
- §2.5 environment persistence — serialized into `lot.json` (unblocked by §4.1).
- §2.4 autosave + crash recovery — now defined here, on the §4.1 writer.
- §5.3 lot UI elements and §3.7 event/timer scripts — persisted via `lot.json` / `scripts/`.
- §6.2 runtime GLTF + texture loading — reads embedded `assets/*.glb` + textures through `LotVFS`.
- §3.12 per-player data slot and §14.7 snapshots — reserved in the same archive/versioning story rather than bolted on later.

