> **§3 resumes here** at §3.5 — the §4 block above sits ahead of it for execution order (Phase 1), not reading order.

### Milestone 3.5 — Collision groups and layers
**Implementation status:** landed and verified headless. `builder/LotCollisionGroups.cs` is the one table: the eight creator-assignable groups (index i owns layer bit `1<<i`, with `Default` at index 0 so an existing part keeps today's layer), a symmetric interaction matrix, and the mask derivation (`MaskForName`). `LotObject` gained `CollisionGroup` + `SetCollisionGroup`/`RefreshCollision`; its body now wears the group's bit as layer and the group's row of the matrix as mask (`SetCanCollide(false)` still parks it on the reserved `NoCollideLayer`, which wins over the group). The three hardcoded masks were replaced: the player body (`CapsuleController`) masks the `Character` row, the drag mover and the drag-plane query (`PartDragController`) use `AllBits`, and `EditorPickMask` spans every group plus the no-collide layer so a non-default or non-collidable part stays selectable/draggable. The matrix is edited under **View → Collision Groups** (`builder/CollisionGroupControls.cs`, same inline-menu pattern as the environment) and written from Lua; `BuilderScene.RefreshCollision()` is the single O(parts) re-sync, run only on an explicit change. It persists as a `collision` block in `lot.json` (`LotArchive.LotDocument.Collision`, set by `LotSave.Write`, re-applied by `ReplaceLotFromArchive`), so a loaded lot keeps its groups and a lot without the block resets to all-collide.
- [x] Per-object collision layer/mask editing (was hardcoded `CollisionLayer = 1` / `CollisionMask = 0` on every part) with named groups
- [x] Which interactions collide is creator-controlled; the matrix write is documented host-authoritative for anything with enforcement impact (§1.3)
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator puts objects into named collision groups and only the intended objects interact.
**Verification:** `CollisionGroupSelfTest` (48 checks, 0 failures) + `LotPropertySelfTest` 39, `LotArchiveSelfTest` 233, `EnvironmentSelfTest` 39, `GizmoSelfTest` 176, `NetSelfTest` 169, `NetIntegrationTest` 59, `EditorWorkflowSelfTest` 82 — all 0 failures, headless under `#if DEBUG` from `BuilderScene._Ready`. The matrix grid's on-screen layout is a rendering outcome checked by eye in the running client.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

### Milestone 3.6 — Constraints and joints
- [x] Hinge and weld constraints between parts, requiring dynamic bodies (parts were static-only)
- [x] Decide how constraints interact with Anchored / CanCollide from §2.3
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator hinges or welds two parts and they hold or pivot correctly in play.
**Implementation status:** landed and verified headless, on the lightweight design agreed for this milestone. Two link kinds only — beyond the §2.3 Texture/Anchored set those are the vocabulary: `builder/LotConstraint.cs` owns the model (a weld or hinge record between two part handles, a hinge's pivot stored part-local, its world-second-side form, the id table and the `constraints` block of `lot.json`), the **Roblox anchor rule** (one anchored part freezes its welded island; the weld that would join two anchored islands goes inactive — deterministic in creation order, so it is replayable) and the JSON shape. `builder/LotConstraintSession.cs` is the dynamic-body layer and joint builder: unanchored parts swap `StaticBody3D` → `RigidBody3D` on session entry (via `LotObject.SetSimulated`, mass from the mesh's volume) and back on exit, and each active record becomes a Godot joint — welds a `Generic6DofJoint3D` with all six axes locked (enabled limit, lower == upper == 0 — Godot has no locked switch), hinges a `HingeJoint3D` whose frame aims its local Z at the chosen world axis, both added as **siblings of the parts they constrain** (a joint parented to a body it links is how half-frozen axes happen). **A simulated body lives at the lot root, not under its part** (`SetSimulated(simulated, host)`), and `SyncVisuals` puppets the part from the body once per physics step — physics owns the body, the part follows it, exactly the capsule controller's visual/body split. The first cut nested the body under the part and never synced, so bodies fell invisibly while the mesh stood still ("unanchored objects are not affected by gravity" reported from the client); the entity-destroy walk now also releases a simulated body explicitly (`ReleaseSimulatedBody`), since it no longer dies with its part. Anchored parts deliberately keep their static body — infinite mass by definition, so the solver never sees them — and the player capsule is excluded outright, since its controller owns it. **The session build hangs off `EnterTestMode`, not off `StartScriptRuntime`** — the script runtime early-returns when Lua is unavailable, and the body swap and joints are physics, so they must exist even with a dead scripting layer. Joints are session artifacts, marked `InternalChildMeta`, rebuilt idempotently whenever a script reload mid-session changes the tree and freed by the entity-destroy walk the moment a linked part dies (a joint to a freed body is a crash). The editor is the same data: Toolbox **Weld**/**Hinge** buttons run a picker (`builder/ConstraintPicker.cs`). Weld is two clicks (part, part). Hinge is: click the first part, click the second part or empty space for the world, then a **placement step** — a visible hinge-point marker (ball + axis rod, internal-marked) appears at the first click's point, further clicks move it (on a surface, or along the camera-facing plane on empty space), and the Toolbox's **Confirm**/**Cancel** buttons finish or abandon it; nothing is created until Confirm. A fresh hinge gets the **swing-friendly default axis** — horizontal, perpendicular to the line between the parts (`ConstraintPicker.DefaultHingeAxis`), because the vertical axis' zero-torque equilibrium reads as "the hinge does nothing" — and the axis rod shows it before Confirm. **The axis is a free world-space direction with sharp tools** (`builder/HingeAxisMath.cs`: exact **Vertical**/**Horizontal** presets and 45° **Tilt**/**Yaw** steps), offered both during placement (Toolbox) and afterwards (Inspector, one history entry per press), and **a hinge marker is drawn for the selected part's hinges in build mode too** (`builder/HingeVisualizer.cs`, pooled internal-marked markers via the shared `ConstraintVisuals`) — which is what makes an orientation, and a change to it, visible without entering Test mode. The axis persists as a v1 letter when it is axis-aligned ("x", "-y", ...) and as a vector otherwise, so old lots and angled hinges both read. Each gesture is one undo entry. The Inspector grows a Constraints section (`builder/ConstraintControls.cs`) with per-link enable/remove and hinge axis/limits/motor widgets, live-applied to the running joint, and welds/hinges are **listed in the Hierarchy** under a `Links (n)` section (`HierarchyPanel.DrawLinksSection`): one row per record, click selects the first part (whose Inspector edits the link), right-click offers Select parts / Toggle / Remove — drawn as a view over the one record table (the same pattern as the base-content section), not as duplicated placeholder nodes that could drift. **Test mode is an instance of the lot** (the §3.4 semantics completed): entering snapshots the authored world (`builder/LotSessionSnapshot.cs` — transforms, colors, visibility and every declared property per entity, UI rects/labels, held texture ids, the constraint and collision-group tables, and the entity set) and leaving restores it after the exit reload, so physics results, script edits and session-spawned nodes cannot leak into the build lot or a later save. Not restored, deliberately: an authored entity a session script *destroyed* (Test mode restores state, not deletions), the camera and the selection. Scripts get eight verbs on the existing bound surface: `WeldParts`, `Unweld`, `IsWelded`, `CreateHinge`, `SetHingeLimits`, `SetHingeMotor`, `SetHingeAxis` (aim the axis at any direction; the string form on `CreateHinge` only spells the six axis directions), `RemoveConstraint`. Reserved and rejected with a readable message rather than half-supported: the servo/angle motor mode (waits on §3.8's tick drive), restitution and break thresholds (waits on §3.7's events). **Long chains warn instead of capping** (the agreed decision): a session counts the active links per welded island and warns above eight (`LotConstraints.ChainWarnThreshold`) — springiness grows with chain length, and the fix (anchor a root, split the build) stays the creator's call. The hinge editor also gave the property system its `Float` kind (decision D3), which §3.11's mass/friction will reuse. **Manual call-out:** `LotObject.CollisionBody` is now typed `PhysicsBody3D` (base of both bodies) instead of `StaticBody3D` — every existing reader compiles unchanged, but anyone who typed it `StaticBody3D` in their own code must widen it too.
**Verification:** `ConstraintSelfTest` (113 checks, 0 failures) covers the hinge basis convention (right-handedness and the Z-aim — for the world axes and an angled one), the sharp axis tools (Vertical/Horizontal presets, 45° Tilt/Yaw steps, zero-axis refusal), the player-push arithmetic (mass-scaled and capped, no push when standing, backing off or already keeping up), the registry and pair-order tolerance, the anchor rule's full truth table, the chain gauge (islands, splits, hinge counting, the warn-not-cap threshold), the `constraints` block through the real JSON encoder/parser incl. the Vector3-as-string pitfall, both axis storage forms (a v1 letter and an angled vector), and clean skips for unknown kind/stale index/bad axis, the body swap and joint build/teardown against a live world (three active links of four records, the split weld absent, node paths resolving to the swapped bodies, live hinge edits), a full save→read→restore→resolve round trip to disk, the Float kind end-to-end (walk capture, JSON, service history, unregistered again afterwards) and the eight Lua verbs through the real VM. A **staged session probe** adds 10 more checks across real frames: it drives the actual `EnterTestMode` entry with a scenario in the lot (a free-falling part, an anchored/unanchored hinge pair, a weld frozen by its anchored side, a weld between two free parts, a floor and a loose part in the character's path) and asserts the full behavior — the free part's visual falls, the hinge's anchored side does not move while its free side swings under gravity and holds its pivot distance, the anchored weld freezes its island, the free weld falls as one with its gap intact, **the character walks forward (driven by a synthetic physical W key — `Input.ParseInputEvent`, the same state the controller reads) and pushes the loose part ahead of it**, and **leaving Test mode restores the authored transform**. It runs headless-only (it would hijack an interactive debug session) and needs `--quit-after 500` so the ~1.4 s of wall-clock runtime after the suite actually happens. The synchronous suite cannot see this class of bug (no physics steps between the swap and the teardown), which is exactly how the first cut shipped bodies that fell invisibly; the probe is the regression for it. Runs headless from `BuilderScene._Process` after the `_Ready` suites (it registers that scratch Float descriptor, so it must not shift their property counts); the on-screen feel of the picker gesture, the hinge-point marker, the build-mode hinge markers and a swinging door are rendering outcomes checked by eye in the running client. **One follow-up fix from §3.7's session probe:** `LotConstraintSession.ApplySimulation` now skips parts that are *queued for deletion* — a node is still in the tree for the rest of its frame, and simulating one created a root-hosted body that its own deletion cannot release (the body is a sibling, not a child), leaving an orphaned collider that blocked the character in the next session (see §3.7's implementation status).
**Difficulty:** 🟠 (was 🔴 until the design settled; the remaining unknowns were the weld representation — Godot has no weld joint — and the Anchored interaction, both settled above)

### Milestone 3.7 — Event/signal system
- [x] Script-subscribed events: `touched` / `touchEnded`, plus per-frame and timer callbacks — scripts today have only load + `start()`, with `Tick()` never reaching Lua. **Delivered: `touched`/`touchEnded` and `onFrame`; `clicked` waits on Phase 2's in-session input path and the timer verbs are §3.8's (they ride this spine).**
- [x] The creator-facing vocabulary is settled once, here (these names are API from this point on): `touched(other, player)` / `touchEnded(other)`, `clicked(player)`, `entered` / `exited` (reserved — §3.13's query volumes raise them through THIS registry, riding the existing event path "instead of a second subscription mechanism"), `destroyed`, `playerJoined` / `playerLeft` (§8.1), and `onFrame(delta)` — the per-frame callback declared like `start()` (a top-level function, zero registration cost, and the first thing a Roblox-native creator reaches for). Stepped-vs-Heartbeat is deliberately collapsed to ONE render-frame point; §3.8's timers fire through this same spine (one scheduler, not two)
- [x] Event registration through the bound API (narrow subscribe/unsubscribe methods per §1.1, never raw Godot signal connections leaking into Lua), stored the way `net.*` already stores handlers: a Lua-side registry keyed by (subject, event) with the callback never retained as a C# wrapper, one bound bookkeeping method behind declaration sugar (the `makeNamespace` pattern, `NetRegister`-shaped) and one stack-based dispatch entry (`__openlot_dispatchEvent`, mirroring `__openlot_dispatch`). Subscribing targets any handle, so one manager script can watch many parts. **Landed as a `SubscribeEvent`/`UnsubscribeEvent` plumbing pair (kept out of the `Lot` table like `NetInvoke`/`NetRegister`) behind a per-env `Subscribe(target, event, fn)` sugar — per-env because the shared read-only `Lot` proxy cannot know which entity is calling (design doc D2).**
- [x] `touched` is **overlap-based, not collision-based**: subscribing opts a part into a sensor, so a `CanCollide = false` trigger plate works by design (that case is the whole point) and a static part works as well as a rigid one; §3.5's matrix governs physics and cannot silently swallow events. `touched`/`touchEnded` are edge-triggered pairs (a resting contact does not re-fire), `other` is the other entity's handle, and `InternalChildMeta` nodes (collision hulls, decal quads, joints) are filtered out so no creator ever receives an implementation handle. **Sensor shapes are padded copies of the part's collision shape (0.15 m), because physics stops a body exactly at contact — touching is not overlapping (design doc D11), and the player's invisible body wears an event-only layer bit so sensors can see it and nothing else can (D12).**
- [x] Deliveries queue during the frame and dispatch only from `ScriptRuntime.Tick` — the shape `NetRouter.Flush()` already has: Lua never runs inside a physics callback, every delivery is ONE watchdog unit so one misbehaving handler trips alone, and the frame budget spans events, net and timers uniformly. The existing 3-trips circuit breaker covers events exactly as it covers `net.*` — one breaker, not two. **Drained before `Router.Flush()` so a handler's `net.*` call dispatches the same frame.**
- [x] Handler storms are bounded by construction: duplicate (subject, event, other) deliveries within one frame coalesce, a per-frame delivery cap drops overflow with ONE throttled `LotLog` line (the aggregate-and-throttle pattern `NetRouter.UnknownHandlerStat` already uses), and event arguments are primitives built on the stack — handles and the player id, no tables, no serializer, so a hot contact path allocates nothing per delivery. **Cap: 128 queue entries per frame; order is subject-ascending then enqueue sequence, and within one (subject, event) declaration order — deterministic for tests (design doc D8/D9).**
- [x] Handlers run under the existing watchdog/memory budgets; one misbehaving handler must not stall the frame (frame-budget accounting in `ScriptRuntime.Tick`)
- [x] Attribution flows through deliveries via the §3.4 path (`BeginPlayerInteraction`): a delivery enters the same dispatch context `net.*` uses, so inside a `touched` handler `net.fromPlayer` is true when the toucher was the local player's own character and false for a physics-caused touch, and the handler's own `net.server` calls inherit it (rule D6). **This half lands with this milestone** — the touch path is physics (`FindCharacter` names the player), not UI input, so it is not the fabricated plumbing §3.4 refuses; what waits is `clicked` and remote-player attribution. **Landed: the player's `player` argument carries the transport's local peer id (loopback = 1, never hardcoded), and the visual capsule's static collider — a shadow of the same entity — is deduped so a character contributes exactly one contact.**
- [ ] `clicked` carries the originating player through the in-session input path, so it lands together with the in-lot command UI (5.2) and lot UI activation (5.3) — one shared in-session input blocker
- [ ] `playerJoined`/`playerLeft` wait on §8.1 (no peers exist on `LoopbackTransport`); the chat UI work in 8.3 can use them once they land
- [x] Subscription lifetime is leak-proof by construction: destroying the subscriber removes its handlers from every subject it subscribed to, and destroying the subject removes what it carries — both through the one `DestroyEntity` → `ForgetEntity` walk; a reload (VM rebuild, Test-mode entry/exit) drops everything through `ClearRegistries`; every subscribe returns a cancel token; a destroyed entity's handler can never fire afterwards. This closes the memory-review checklist item about subscriptions that outlive their object
- [x] Subscriptions ship inside the lot: event/timer declarations are ordinary Lua in the archive's `scripts/` (§4.1) and load through the same `ScriptRuntime` path — no out-of-band registration file a saved lot could lose. Nothing new enters `lot.json`; a loaded lot re-establishes subscriptions exactly the way it re-declares `net.*`
- [x] Docs before code, at §3.2's bar: `docs/design/milestone-3.7-event-system.md` settles the registration sugar (ONE public mechanism; token cancel; redeclaration replaces within a script, appends across scripts), touch semantics, two-ended cleanup, delivery order (handle-ascending, then declaration order — deterministic for tests), the coalesce/cap numbers, and the attribution matrix; the creator-facing half lands in `docs/net-api.md` or its own file. **Landed as `docs/design/milestone-3.7-event-system.md` (decisions D1–D17 + execution flow + limitations) and `docs/event-api.md` (creator-facing).**
**Implementation status:** landed and verified headless. The spine is `builder/LotEventRegistry.cs` (subscriptions keyed subject→event with declaration-order bookkeeping, the per-frame queue with coalescing and the 128-entry cap, the sensor attach/retry/refuse protocol, two-ended `ForgetEntity`/`ClearAll`); `LuaBootstrap` carries the per-env `Subscribe`/cancel-token sugar plus `__openlot_dispatchEvent`, `__openlot_captureFrame`/`__openlot_hasFrame`/`__openlot_callFrame`; `LuaNetBridge.TryDispatchEvent`/`CallOnFrame` run one armed watchdog unit each through the shared 3-trip breaker; `LotObject.EnsureEventSensor`/`ReleaseEventSensor` build a padded `Area3D` on `LotCollisionGroups.EventSensorMask` (every group + the no-collide layer + the player's event-only `CharacterBodyBit`, so a `CanCollide = false` plate works by design and the invisible player body is detectable by sensors and by nothing else — its layer, mask and every existing pick/drag mask are unchanged); `ScriptRuntime.Tick(delta)` drains before `Router.Flush()` and runs `onFrame` per entity. Sensors are session artifacts (built on entry or when a late subject resolves, torn down on exit); events and `onFrame` are session-gated (design doc D1). Attribution rides `BeginPlayerInteraction` plus the transport's local peer id, with the character's visual-collider shadow deduped. **The session probe also caught and fixed a §3.6 leak on the way:** `LotConstraintSession.ApplySimulation` simulated parts that were *queued for deletion* but still present for the rest of the frame, and a part's own deletion cannot release a root-hosted body (it is a sibling, not a child) — the orphaned rigid body then blocked the character in the next session. `ApplySimulation` now skips `IsQueuedForDeletion()` nodes.
**Verification:** `EventSelfTest` — 47 synchronous checks (registry semantics, the sensor protocol, the Lua sugar on a private VM: replace-within-script vs append-across-scripts, cancel tokens, `net.fromPlayer`, disabled entities, `onFrame` capture/trip/disable, destroyed-subject silence, the reserved vocabulary) plus an 11-check staged session probe: a falling ball delivers exactly one `touched` while it rests and a `touchEnded` when lifted away; the walking character delivers a player-attributed `touched` with `net.fromPlayer` true and the loopback peer id; `onFrame` advances and stops at session exit; destroying the subject drops both its registrations and its sensor; every sensor comes down with the session. Full headless run: all suites green — Event 47 + probe 11 alongside Gizmo 228, Net 170, integration 60, editor workflow 82, constraint 113 + probe 10 and the rest, 0 failures. The on-screen side of a touch is the script's own Output-window line (events are invisible by design), checked by eye in the running client, the project's usual split.
**Acceptance criteria:** A part's script reacts to touch and touchEnded with the other entity's handle, a destroyed entity's handlers stop firing with nothing left in either registry, a player-caused touch handler sees `net.fromPlayer` true in Test mode while a physics-caused one sees false, an `onFrame` callback runs every frame and stops at session exit, a one-frame touch storm is coalesced/capped into bounded work with one throttled log line, a timer callback fires on schedule without tripping the watchdog, and a player-triggered `clicked` handler executes with player attribution (the clicked half lands in Phase 2 with 5.2/5.3).
**Difficulty:** 🟠 registration + the delivery spine · 🟠 touch sensing + bounds · 🟠 lifecycle (tokens, two-ended cleanup) · ⛔️ `clicked` + remote-player attribution (waits on §5.2/§5.3 · §8.1) · ⛔️ `playerJoined`/`playerLeft` (waits on §8.1)

### Milestone 3.8 — Tween/timing library
- [x] Smooth property changes from Lua (position, rotation, color, UI rects) without per-frame loops in scripts
- [x] Implemented as a C# tween list advanced in `ScriptRuntime.Tick`, writing through the existing Lua-accessible setters — Lua never runs per frame, so the watchdog budgets are untouched
- [x] Delayed/repeating timers with cancel handles, surfaced through the same tick rather than Godot's `SceneTreeTimer` (keeps determinism across the reload-on-Test-mode boundary from 3.4)
**Implementation status:** landed and verified headless. `builder/LotScheduler.cs` is the spine: every running tween and timer on one accumulated-delta clock (simulation time, so it pauses exactly with the session), with `builder/LotScheduler.cs`'s `LotEasing` as the fixed pure-math curve set (`linear` + sine/quad/cubic × in/out/in-out, exact endpoints). The five tween verbs (`TweenPosition`/`TweenRotation`/`TweenScale`/`TweenColor`/`TweenUIRect`) and the per-env `After`/`Every` sugar (the `Subscribe` pattern — subscriber identity is the env's handle, the callback stays in a Lua-side registry) all return **cancel tokens**; every call funnels into three hidden plumbing methods (`ScheduleTween`/`ScheduleTimer`/`CancelScheduled`, kept out of the `Lot` table like `NetInvoke`). Timer callbacks fire as one armed watchdog unit each through `LuaNetBridge.CallTimer`, sharing the 3-trip breaker with net/events. Rails: 256-entry cap (refused with one `LotLog` line), repeating timers fire at most once per frame, a freed tween target or a destroyed entity drops its entries (`Scheduler.ForgetEntity` sits next to the event registry's in `CleanupRecursive`), and every reload clears the schedule. UI rects go through the same clamped writer `SetUIRect` uses, so a tween cannot leave the picture frame. Design doc `docs/design/milestone-3.8-tweens-timers.md`; creator doc `docs/tween-api.md`.
**Verification:** `TweenSelfTest` — 57 synchronous checks (every easing curve's endpoints exact / midpoints hand-computed / monotonic; quarter-point interpolation on a hand-advanced clock; zero-duration and all refusal paths; color + clamped UI-rect writes; timer due/exact-once/big-delta-once/next-interval; cancels; the cap; two-ended cleanup incl. a freed target) + a 9-check staged session probe: a script's 10 m tween sampled mid-flight (x 159.15 at 0.7 s of a `quadOut`), landing exactly on 160; a one-shot timer; an `Every` that cancels itself from inside its own third fire (three fires, no fourth); an immediately self-cancelled timer that never fires; the schedule empty once resolved; authored position restored at session exit; destroying the animated entity clearing its schedule. Full headless run: all suites green — Tween 57 + probe 9 alongside Event 47 + probe 11, Gizmo 228, Net 170, integration 60, editor workflow 82, constraint 113 + probe 10 and the rest, 0 failures (952 checks).
**Acceptance criteria:** A script tweens a part across the lot and schedules a delayed callback with one call each, with no per-frame Lua and no watchdog trips.
**Difficulty:** 🟡/🟠 (a C# list plus tick integration; the design constraint is the watchdog, not the math)

### Milestone 3.9 — Input API
- [x] Key/mouse/gamepad state readable from scripts, polled once per frame and cached (not one Lua↔C# call per key — the boundary-crossing cost the perf rules flag)
- [x] Composes with §5.5 keybinding customization and §14.1 gamepad support: scripts see logical actions, never hardcoded physical keys (today `CapsuleController`/`FreecamController` hardcode WASD)
- [x] Authority rule documented: raw input is client-local; anything with enforcement impact goes through host-authoritative `net.server` (§1.3) — decided here, not later
**Implementation status:** landed and verified headless. `builder/LotInputActions.cs` wraps Godot's InputMap (design doc D1): seven logical actions (`moveForward/Backward/Left/Right`, `jump`, `sprint`, `interact`) registered once with default events — the exact keys the controllers already used (WASD/Space/Shift/E) plus gamepad basics (left stick, A, X, left shoulder) — and one `Poll()` per frame in `BuilderScene._Process` (every mode, so the freecam works in build mode and even when the Lua runtime is unavailable) fills a held/pressed/released/strength snapshot with just-pressed and just-released true for exactly one frame. Mouse motion and wheel accumulate in `BuilderScene._Input` and are snapshotted by the same poll. Six creator reads on `LotLuaApi` — `IsActionPressed`, `WasActionPressed`, `WasActionReleased`, `GetActionStrength`, `GetMouseDeltaX/Y`, `GetMouseWheel` — answer purely from the snapshot, are **session-gated** (outside a session they answer false/0, so editor keystrokes never leak into scripts), and warn on unknown names. Both controllers now read the action layer with identical defaults (D5), so §5.5's rebinding will move the character, the freecam and every script together; `Rebind` is in-memory here and §5.5 owns persistence + the settings UI. Design doc `docs/design/milestone-3.9-input-api.md`; creator doc `docs/input-api.md`.
**Verification:** `InputSelfTest` — the spike that the whole milestone rests on (a synthetic `Input.ParseInputEvent` physical W drives the InputMap action headless — with `FlushBufferedEvents`, the buffered-input quirk the spike caught), the action vocabulary and snapshot edge detection (pressed for exactly one frame, held across frames, released once, strength pass-through, out-of-range reads), the mouse accumulation snapshot — plus a 5-check staged session probe: the script saw `moveForward` held while W was down (strength > 0), the character **physically walked** (z 4 → -2.45 — the controller-refactor regression), a live `Rebind` moved the action to Q mid-session while W no longer applied, and a Space tap produced exactly one `WasActionPressed("jump")` frame. Full headless run: all suites green — Input 13 + probe 5 alongside Tween 57 + probe 9, Event 47 + probe 11, Gizmo 228, Net 170, integration 60, editor workflow 82, constraint 113 + probe 10, and the rest — 0 failures (970 checks).
**Acceptance criteria:** A script reads jump/interact actions each frame via logical names, works with rebound keys and a gamepad, and a hostile client cannot move another player's character.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 (+ 🔴 if input ever drives enforcement — see §1.3)

### Milestone 3.10 — Camera API
- [x] Script-selectable modes: first-person, fixed, and scripted (cutscene) — built as modes on the existing freecam/orbital machinery (`OrbitalCamera` seeding + the shared-input handoff already exist), not a second camera stack
- [x] Cutscene scripting writes through per-frame C# interpolation (same tick pattern as 3.8), so Lua describes the shot and C# moves the camera
- [x] Handoff rule with Test-mode entry/exit (3.4) and character attach/detach documented — which owner holds the camera in each state
**Implementation status:** landed and verified headless. `builder/LotCameraMath.cs` holds the pure parts (mode parse/round-trip, the first-person eye math); `OrbitalCamera` gained the mode machine — third person is byte-for-byte the old behaviour, first person parks at the character's eye (`target + 1.6`) on the same look input while `BuilderScene.ApplyCameraModeVisibility` hides the character's own mesh, fixed holds a creator-set pose (a side never set is captured from the current pose, so "switch to fixed" means "hold where you are" first), and a shot advances on the frame delta through §3.8's easing engine and returns to the previous mode. `Lot.SetCameraMode` / `SetCameraPosition` / `SetCameraTarget` / `FlyCamera` / `CancelCameraShot` / `GetCameraMode` are the creator surface (the bootstrap wraps FlyCamera in a cancel token; `"scripted"` is refused directly — FlyCamera is the only way in). Cancel converts the current pose into the fixed one ("the camera simply stops"); a natural end returns to the previous mode instead. Everything is session-only: entry always starts in third person with the character visible, exit resets it, and nothing enters `lot.json` (a view, not an edit). **One behaviour refinement of §3.4's handoff, made deliberate by this milestone:** `PlayerCameraActive` now gates only LOOK input — the camera's pose updates whenever the camera is `Current`, so a modal can deny input without freezing/detaching the view, and the headless probes (where the ImGui-derived gate never resolves) can verify every mode; the §3.4 authority truth table is untouched. Design doc `docs/design/milestone-3.10-camera-api.md`; creator doc `docs/camera-api.md`.
**Verification:** `CameraSelfTest` — 9 synchronous checks (mode parse/refuse/round-trip, the first-person eye offset, and the Lua shot sugar on a private VM: token cancel with the right shot id, unknown easing and negative duration raising) + a 14-check staged session probe: a real script switches to first person (camera at the eye (4, 2.63, 4), mesh hidden) → fixed (exactly (30,5,30), mesh visible again) → flies a shot (scripted mode, x 55.9 strictly between 30 and 60 mid-flight) → the shot ends back in fixed → a second shot is cancelled mid-flight and the camera **freezes** (stable at (24.0, 5, 24.0) across frames, i.e. neither origin nor target) → leaving the session returns the freecam and the next entry starts from third person with the character visible. Full headless run: all suites green — Camera 9 + probe 14 alongside Input 13 + probe 5, Tween 57 + probe 9, Event 47 + probe 11, Gizmo 228, Net 170, integration 60, editor workflow 82, constraint 113 + probe 10, and the rest — 0 failures (993 checks).
**Acceptance criteria:** A script switches a player to first-person, pins a fixed camera, then plays a scripted cutscene move and returns control cleanly.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

### Milestone 3.11 — Physics API
- [ ] Raycasts from Lua (the query path already exists internally in `PartDragController` — this is exposure, not new physics)
- [ ] Forces/velocity on parts — builds on the dynamic-body layer §3.6 shipped (unanchored parts simulate in a session); this is exposure of `ApplyForce`/velocity through the bound API
- [ ] Material properties per part (friction, bounciness, mass) on the bodies §3.6 shipped; the `Float` property kind §3.6 added is what will carry them, so the Inspector and Lua agree on vocabulary
**Acceptance criteria:** A script raycasts to find what's ahead, pushes a dynamic part with a force, and sets friction/bounciness that observably change how it slides and bounces.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 raycasts · 🟠 forces/velocity (builds on §3.6's landed dynamic layer) · 🟡 material properties once bodies exist

### Milestone 3.12 — Per-player persistent data API (DataStore equivalent)
- [ ] Design doc before code (same bar as 3.2): where the data lives under P2P hosting (§8.2) — host-held with client cache vs. each-peer-owns-own — settled before any storage code
- [ ] Key/value per-player store with read/write through the bound API; enforcement-relevant reads stay host-authoritative (§1.3)
- [ ] On-disk slot reserved in the §4.1 `.lot` archive for player data (its own entry alongside `lot.json` / `scripts/`), decided with the format rather than bolted on later
**Acceptance criteria:** A script saves a player's coins, the value survives a lot reload, and the ownership model is documented — no silent "client tells the host its balance" trust hole.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 storage · 🔴 ownership design (decide early)

### Milestone 3.13 — Spatial query primitives
*The primitive behind area triggers, melee hitboxes and explosion radiuses: a region asks "what is inside me", and every other shape of query is built on that answer.*
- [ ] Query shapes — sphere and box overlap plus shape casts (the raycast path already exists internally in `PartDragController`; this is exposure plus the missing shapes) — callable from Lua through the bound API (§1.1) and from the editor
- [ ] Placeable **query volume objects**: an insertable object (`LotObjectKind.QueryVolume`, spawned from the Toolbox like a Capsule, §3.4) that runs its query each tick and reports what is inside/overlapping, so a creator places a trigger the way they place a part
- [ ] Results come back as handles/part references, never raw Godot objects, and are batched once per frame rather than one Lua↔C# crossing per hit (§2.4 boundary-crossing rule)
- [ ] Broad-phase before narrow-phase: candidates are filtered by AABB + collision layer/mask (§3.5) before exact shape tests, so a large-radius query over many parts stays cheap
- [ ] Feeds the §3.7 event system — an overlap volume's enter/exit result is what raises `touched` / `entered` / `exited`, so area triggers ride the existing event path instead of a second subscription mechanism
- [ ] Melee-hitbox and explosion-radius helpers built on the same primitive (radial query + distance falloff), not separate one-off paths; anything that *pushes* what it finds rides §3.6's landed dynamic layer, exactly like §3.11's forces
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator places a query volume, a script reads which parts are inside it each frame, a melee hitbox reports the parts it swept, and an explosion reports everything within its radius — all through the same query API, with no per-hit Lua call storm.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 query shapes + volume object · 🟠 result batching · 🟠 push-on-hit (rides §3.6's landed dynamic layer)

