## 5. Terminal / Menu UI

*Status: functionally complete from the Unity prototype; migration to Godot in progress (main menu + terminal already moved and fixed up as of today).*

### Milestone 5.1 — terminal system part 2
- [ ] Re-verify command set parity: help, openlot (join/host/list/create), clear, neofetch, sysinfo, motd, ping, matrix, register/login/logout, theme, alias, ls, avatar, disconnect, registertopublic, wallpaper (added by §2.3)
- [ ] Re-verify auth gate (login/register/help/clear open pre-login, everything else locked)
- [ ] Re-verify "openlot list" interactive TUI selection flow into a lot
**Acceptance criteria:** Every command from the Unity terminal build works identically in the Godot port, auth gating behaves the same, and TUI list navigation loads a selected lot.
**Difficulty:** 🟩/🟠 (Rolkka-owned, feel/fit-and-finish)

### Milestone 5.2 — In-lot command UI
- [ ] Design and build an in-game window/UI system for commands while inside a lot (the old TerminalOutput UI was removed from LocalLot for being buggy there — needs a proper replacement, not a reuse)
**Acceptance criteria:** A player/creator can open a command UI while inside a live lot without the bugs that broke the previous in-lot terminal attempt.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

### Milestone 5.3 — Lot UI customization
- [ ] More customization options for lot UI elements (`LotUIRoot`), using the same typed property widgets as 2.3
- [ ] Checkbox and dropdown controls available as lot UI elements, not only in the editor (new `LotUIKind`s — today it is Frame/Button/Text/Scrollbar)
- [ ] Scrollable containers for lot UI, using ImGui's existing child-region support (`BeginChild`) for clipping and scrolling rather than the current `Scrollbar` kind, which draws a static thumb over nothing
- [ ] Click routing that reaches script handlers: element presses resolved through ImGui item state inside the viewport window, extended past today's editor-only selection/drag path in `UiGizmoController` to actual activation during a player session
- [ ] Customization properties settable from Lua
*Note: the click-routing and scroll-container items share the blocker with the in-lot command UI in 5.2 and the player-originated `net.*` attribution item in 3.4 — the ImGui primitives exist, but there is no in-session path from a lot UI press to a script handler yet. The new element kinds and the whole lot UI tree are persisted through §4.1's `lot.json`, so a saved lot carries its UI without a second format.*
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator can build a lot UI panel with a checkbox, a dropdown and a scroll area, style it, and have a script react to its values.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 (ImGui supplies scrolling and item-state hit-testing; the work is element kinds, property wiring, and extending routing to in-session activation)

### Milestone 5.4 — Client settings system
- [ ] Settings screen grown from the existing theme-only window (`Terminal.DrawSettingsWindow`) into a real, tabbed settings surface
- [ ] Camera settings: FOV, mouse sensitivity (already fields on `FreecamController` / `OrbitalCamera`, currently hardcoded to 0.15), invert-Y, and a scope decision (per-camera or global)
- [ ] Graphics settings: resolution scale / viewport size, shadow toggle, MSAA, render method — applied live where Godot allows it
- [ ] Window styles: a shared window-chrome pass on top of `EditorChrome.PanelWindowFlags` — window rounding, border size/colour, padding, title-bar alignment, frame/item spacing — pushed once at the top of every layout pass and popped at its end (`Terminal.OnLayout` + `Builder.DrawLayout`) through the wrapper's existing `PushStyleVar` / `PushStyleColor` (`ImGui.State.cs` already exposes `StyleVarWindowRounding`, `StyleVarWindowPadding`, `ColWindowBg`, `ColTitleBg` and the rest), so the terminal windows, builder panels and dialogs wear one consistent chrome instead of today's default ImGui look. **Per-window overrides and per-lot creator styles are deliberately out of scope** — player-facing lot UI styling is §5.3's territory, not the client theme's
- [ ] Fonts and themes: one shared theme service owning the palette, the window style set above and the fonts together — UI font selection (the ImGui wrapper already exposes `AddFontFromFile` / `PushFont` / `MergeIconFontFromFile`, so this is selection + persistence, not new plumbing) — today's two palettes are hardcoded terminal-local `Color` fields (`Terminal.ApplyGreenMatrixTheme` / `ApplyAmberRetroTheme`), so this lifts them into one theme applied across the terminal, builder panels and lot UI instead of per-surface colours. One global theme, not per-surface ones, so the terminal and build mode can never drift apart
- [ ] Preset themes (Green Matrix, Amber Retro, plus a neutral default) with a custom palette and window-shape option set, previewed live rather than apply-and-see
- [ ] Settings persisted to disk and applied on startup, with a restore-defaults action — the theme, window-style and font blocks land in the existing `user://openlot_settings.cfg` as new `[theme]` / `[window_style]` / `[font]` sections next to `[wallpaper] source_path`, rather than a second config format
- [ ] Settings reachable in-lot as well as from the main menu, so a player can change them without leaving a session
**Acceptance criteria:** A player can open settings from the main menu or in-lot, change FOV/sensitivity/graphics options, pick a font and theme, adjust window rounding/border/padding and title styling, see them take effect immediately across the terminal and builder, and find them still applied after restarting the client.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 FOV/sensitivity/persistence · 🟠 graphics options · 🟠 fonts/themes/window styles · 🟠 in-lot access
**Verification:** a headless round-trip self-test on the theme service (parse → apply → persist → reload, the same shape as `WallpaperSelfTest`) pins the config contract; the on-screen result (colours, rounding, borders, font metrics) is a rendering outcome checked by eye in the running client, the project's usual split.

### Milestone 5.5 — Keybinding customization
- [ ] Rebindable keys for editor tools, camera and player controls, with conflict detection and a restore-defaults action (feeds §5.4's settings screen)
**Acceptance criteria:** A player rebinds a key, and both the editor and in-lot controls use the new binding after a restart.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

### Milestone 5.6 — Tiling window modes (Hyprland-style)
*Windows are free-floating today. Docking was deliberately turned off (`EditorChrome`) because the dock-node double-click "maximize" gesture cannot be disabled from the wrapper, and the accepted cost was "panels can no longer be dragged into dock splits". This milestone gives that arrangement back without re-enabling docking: OpenLot computes every tile itself each layout pass and hands windows their rect via `SetNextWindowPos` / `SetNextWindowSize` (`CondAlways`), so the broken gesture cannot return and the vendored GDExtension needs no patch. The headline arrangement is **viewport focus** — the 3D view owns the master tile so most of the screen is the scene, with the panels stacked along the edge — plus Hyprland's signature gaps and focus border riding on §5.4's window-style service.*
- [ ] Global mode toggle — **Floating** (today: windows drag, resize and persist geometry in `user://imgui.ini`) ↔ **Tiled** (the layout owns geometry; windows run `NoMove` / `NoResize` while tiled and `TitleBarWindowDrag` is bypassed rather than allowed to fight the computed rect). The bottom command strip (`BuilderCommandLine`) is not a tile — it keeps its fixed full-width slot
- [ ] Per-window float toggle (the `togglefloating` equivalent): any single window can pop back to floating while the rest stay tiled, from the View menu — and from the window itself if a title-bar affordance proves clean, since `WindowNoCollapse` already spends the title-bar double-click on nothing
- [ ] Layout presets — at minimum **Viewport focus** (master = the 3D viewport at a default ~0.7 ratio; secondary column = Toolbox / Inspector / Hierarchy / Output, plus the Code Editor when open) and an even two-column split; the master ratio is adjustable by dragging the tile divider, which is the standard resize gesture because window grips are off in tiled mode
- [ ] Hyprland-flavoured trimmings on top of §5.4: gaps between tiles (size setting, 0 = off), rounded corners and border size/colour from the shared window-style set, plus a focus-coloured border on the active tile
- [ ] Applies to builder panels and terminal windows alike (`Builder.DrawLayout` and `Terminal.OnLayout` resolve the same layout); modals, dialogs and file pickers never tile — they stay floating above the layout — and the wallpaper's under-dockspace window is untouched
- [ ] **Floating geometry survives a tiled stint**: tiled rects must not overwrite the remembered floating positions (e.g. tiles run `WindowNoSavedSettings` while tiled so the last floating rect stays in `imgui.ini`); toggling back finds windows where they were, not in their tiles
- [ ] Tiling is client preference, never lot state: mode, preset, gap and per-window float flags persist in a `[layout]` section of `user://openlot_settings.cfg` beside §5.4's blocks. Deliberately out of scope in the first cut: animated tile transitions (instant geometry per frame) and keyboard-driven tiling commands (that would be §5.5 territory if ever wanted)
- [ ] Game mode is untouched (its viewport is already fullscreen and the editor chrome is not drawn), and the standing interactions must work as well tiled as floating: the hierarchy's marquee and drag-drop, the viewport's click-claiming, and the 3D `SubViewport` following its computed tile size the same way it follows a manual resize today
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator toggles tiling on, the viewport takes most of the screen with the panels tiled around it, drags the divider to rebalance, floats one window back out, toggles everything back to floating and finds the free positions exactly as they were, and the choices survive a restart.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 layout engine + gesture interplay · 🟡 presets / gaps / persistence
**Verification:** the layout math (per-preset rect derivation, master ratio, gap application, float bookkeeping) as a headless self-test in the `*SelfTest` pattern — pure geometry, fully testable without eyes; the on-screen feel (gaps, focus border, divider drag) is a rendering outcome checked by eye in the running client, the project's usual split.

---

## 6. Characters, 3D meshes

*Capsule prototyping moved to §3.4 — it is a runtime concern (Test mode needs a controllable character to be meaningful), not an art/asset one.*

### Milestone 6.1 — Rigged characters
- [ ] R6-style rig support
- [ ] Body part tagging
- [ ] .avlot format: accessories/3D mesh attachment
- [ ] .avlot format: animation tagging (walking/jumping/etc. — untagged animations won't function properly)
**Acceptance criteria:** A rigged character loads with attached accessories and plays a tagged walk/jump animation correctly triggered by movement state.
**Difficulty:** 🔴 R6 rig · 🟠 accessories/animation tagging

### Milestone 6.2 — Runtime GLTF/rigged mesh loading/ Accessory application
- [ ] Runtime loading of rigged 3D meshes (GLTF) with custom textures for characters/accessories
- [ ] Confirm GLTFDocument/GLTFState API + Load() pattern works end-to-end for OpenLot's asset pipeline
- [ ] Route loading through **`LotVFS` (§4.1)**: the `.glb` and its textures arrive as buffers out of the lot archive's `assets/` (`GltfDocument.AppendFromBuffer` + `GltfState`), never as loose files on disk — the pack the archive ships is the pack the runtime reads, and this is the seam §14.6's asset browser later plugs into
- [ ] Character Edit UI (Inside game creation Environment) Accessory placement, 2d clothing.
**Acceptance criteria:** A character rig with a custom texture loads at runtime from a GLTF file with no editor pre-import step.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 

