# POST MLP

## 10. Mobile Support (Android)

*OpenLot on phones and tablets. The core is portable — Godot's Android export carries the renderer, physics and the same C#/Lua runtime, and the network path is §8's session model unchanged, so mobile is a client, not a new transport. What is **not** portable is the interaction model: every surface today assumes mouse hover, a right mouse button, a keyboard and freely positioned windows, and a touchscreen has none of those. Sits ahead of §11 by design — player reach on Android is a distribution question, the plugin tiers are a creator-ecosystem one — and the real work is the restructuring below, not the export.*

### Milestone 10.1 — Android client build & touch input foundation
- [ ] Android export of the player client (arm64; the renderer choice — Forward+/Mobile on Vulkan vs the Compatibility (GLES3) path — decided and documented against real device coverage, not assumed)
- [ ] Touch enters through the §3.9 input abstraction (this milestone depends on it landing): on-screen movement (virtual stick or touch-steer), camera look by drag, and jump/interact buttons map to the same logical actions a keyboard binds to — scripts and the §3.4 capsule controller keep seeing logical actions only
- [ ] Device basics: safe-area/notch insets, DPI-aware sizing (the `DisplaySetup` content-scale decision revisited for touch), and mobile defaults for §5.4's graphics settings (resolution scale, shadows, MSAA) chosen once and persisted like any other setting
**Acceptance criteria:** The default lot is playable on an Android phone with touch alone — spawn, move, look, jump — with no desktop input assumed anywhere on the way in.
**Difficulty:** ⛔️ waits on §3.9's input abstraction · 🟠 export + input mapping · 🟡 device basics

### Milestone 10.2 — Touch-first UI restructuring
*The touchscreen feel is the real work: the editor-style UI assumes hover, right-click and precise clicking everywhere. This is a restructuring pass over the shared ImGui surfaces — one UI system (§1.2) — not a second mobile UI.*
- [ ] No hover-dependent affordance survives on touch paths: hover states get tap equivalents, right-click context menus answer to long-press, hit targets meet finger sizes (a touch sizing set in §5.4's window-style service, not per-surface tweaks), and gestures that fight scrolling are resolved per surface
- [ ] Window model: free-floating windows do not fit a phone — reuse §5.6's computed-tile layout as a one-column full-screen stack with per-surface switching, so the same layout engine that tiles the desktop stacks the phone
- [ ] Software keyboard: terminal command entry, chat (§8.3), text fields and search raise the IME without corrupting the ImGui layout, and dismissing it restores focus
- [ ] Player-facing surfaces get their touch passes: in-lot UI (§5.3, including touch-friendly element sizing and the scroll containers), the in-lot command UI (§5.2), chat (§8.3) and in-lot settings (§5.4)
- [ ] Creation-environment scope decided and documented (decided here, not assumed in code): recommended first cut is settings + terminal + chat touch-workable, with the 3D build mode desktop-only until it earns its own milestone
**Acceptance criteria:** A player on a phone navigates menus, opens the terminal and runs a command with the on-screen keyboard, joins a lot, moves their character, chats and changes settings — with no surface unreachable because it required hover or right-click.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 restructuring pass · 🟠 window model reuse · 🟠 software keyboard (IME)

### Milestone 10.3 — Android release readiness
- [ ] Lifecycle: background/resume without dropping the session (pause-menu semantics while backgrounded), orientation handling, and battery/thermal-aware frame pacing
- [ ] Packaging: signed APK/AAB, build versioning tied to the client build, and a device matrix (screen sizes, DPI, GPU tiers, paired controllers via §14.1) recorded with results
**Acceptance criteria:** A signed build installs from the artifact onto a fresh device, backgrounds and resumes mid-session, and the matrix records what passed on which hardware.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 lifecycle · 🟡 packaging

