## 12. Benchmarks

*Two jobs: keep the project honest to itself, and back its central claim. The repo's performance-review rules ("no per-frame allocations", "avoid bloat") only bite when there are numbers to check them against, and the lightweight-versus-Unity/Roblox-bloat thesis is an assertion until it is measured — credibility that matters before §13's provider economy puts those claims in front of paying users.*

### Milestone 12.1 — Benchmark harness
- [ ] One command produces the numbers on any machine — headless, extending the existing headless/`--quit-after` test pattern, no new dependency: startup-to-menu, lot load + save (§4.2) for a standard scene, a fixed N-part physics scene's frame time, Lua↔C# dispatch throughput through the §3.2 bridge, build-mode editor frame time, and memory (managed heap + process RSS)
- [ ] Deterministic and repeatable: fixed scenes, fixed frame counts, fixed seed; results print as a table and are stored per run so a regression between builds shows as a diff — raw numbers plus the machine description, since absolute numbers are hardware-relative
**Acceptance criteria:** A maintainer runs one command and gets today's numbers; two builds' results diff cleanly enough to see a regression; every workload is documented next to the harness.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 deterministic workloads + result storage

### Milestone 12.2 — Published comparison numbers
- [ ] A methodology-documented comparison against a Unity/Roblox baseline on the same workloads and hardware — measuring what the respective licences permit and stating plainly what could not be measured rather than estimating it
- [ ] Published (site/README) and refreshed at each release; the rules stated once: same hardware, same scene, raw numbers plus methodology, no cherry-picking
**Acceptance criteria:** A published table exists whose method a sceptic can repeat; OpenLot's own materials cite it instead of asserting.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 methodology + baseline measurement

---

## 13. Providers & Platform Economy

*Separate from free peer-to-peer hosting (§8.2), which remains available.*

### Milestone 13.1 — Provider system core
- [ ] Provider listing/discovery mechanism (third parties list games on their own discoverable list)
- [ ] Provider-managed hosting path as an alternative to self-hosting
- [ ] Per-provider configurable ToS, pricing, rules, moderation policy, age-safety tier
- [ ] Liability model: explicitly provider-owned, not OpenLot-owned, reflected in docs/ToS templates
**Acceptance criteria:** A third-party provider can register, set their own rules/pricing/age-tier, and list/host a game, with OpenLot's own docs clearly scoping liability to the provider.
**Difficulty:** 🔴 new · ⭕️ largely undesigned below the concept level

### Milestone 13.2 — Official OpenLot provider
- [ ] Stand up Rolkka's own instance as the official provider
- [ ] Self-custody USDC flow for creator payouts (wallet-to-wallet, no provider custody)
- [ ] No minor monetization at launch — minors earn/buy only through a parent-held wallet
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator on the official provider can receive a USDC payment directly to their own wallet with the provider never holding funds in the loop.
**Difficulty:** 🔴 (custody-avoidant design already settled; implementation still open)

### Milestone 13.3 — Monetization features (official provider)
- [ ] Managed hosting tiers (bandwidth/storage tiers, priority slots)
- [ ] Discovery/promoted placement in game listings
- [ ] Creator "Pro" subscription tier (extra plugin slots, higher limits, analytics)
- [ ] Player "Plus" subscription tier (cosmetic perks, exclusive access)
- [ ] (Later stage) B2B "OpenLot Cloud" hosted backend infra + consulting for third-party providers
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator can subscribe to Pro and immediately see raised limits/analytics access; a player can subscribe to Plus and receive a cosmetic perk.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 hosting tiers/subscriptions · ⛔️ B2B infra (waits on the provider ecosystem existing — post-MLP)

### Milestone 13.4 — Official plugins (post-MLP stage)
- [ ] Ko-fi / Patreon API integration plugin for gamepasses/paid games
- [ ] Roblox compatibility tools, staged least-to-most advanced (Lua API shim scoping, R6/R15 rig mapping research, asset importer)
- [ ] "Bender" — precise object-bending creation tool
- [ ] "EZLot" — official UI navigation shell over the terminal command surface (`openlot join/host/list`, theme, wallpaper, avatar, register/login, …): every command gets a clickable equivalent so the whole app is comfortably navigable without typing; built as a Tier 1 plugin (terminal/window-API-only, §1.4) so it doubles as the reference proof that third-party UI apps need no core access — the terminal stays as the power-user path
- [ ] (More TBD as MLP usage reveals gaps)
**Acceptance criteria:** Ko-fi/Patreon plugin lets a creator gate a paid game/gamepass without OpenLot ever touching payment custody; Roblox compat tier list published even if only partial shimming is implemented; a user who never opens the terminal joins/hosts/lists lots and changes theme/wallpaper/avatar entirely through EZLot.
**Difficulty:** 🟡 Ko-fi/Patreon · 🔴 Roblox compat (start scoping/research early, don't wait for "last stage" to begin) · 🟠 Bender · 🟠 EZLot shell (UI over existing commands)

