## Implementation Index — Milestone 3.2 (`net.*` + per-entity scripts)

Full decision record, execution flow and limitations: **`docs/design/milestone-3.2-unified-script-model.md`**.
Creator-facing reference: **`docs/net-api.md`**.

**Where the pieces live** (all under `builder/`):

| Piece | File | One-line contract |
|---|---|---|
| VM bootstrap + sandbox | `LuaBootstrap.cs` | The Lua chunk: default-deny whitelist, sealed metatables, `makeNet(handle)`, validation pre-pass, watchdog hook, env loader, cross-entity sugar. Everything here is sugar over the bound `LotLuaApi` methods — no Godot access from Lua (§1.1). |
| Watchdog | `LuaWatchdog.cs` | Instruction budgets (200k/dispatch, 1M/script load, 4M/frame, 3 trips ⇒ circuit breaker). Hook installed **from Lua**; always cleared **from C#** first. |
| Memory guard | `LuaMemoryGuard.cs` | `lua_setallocf` hard cap (64 MB), `OomHit` flag checked after every dispatch, VM rebuilt at a frame boundary. |
| Net bridge | `LuaNetBridge.cs` | `IDispatchTarget`: decode args onto the raw Lua stack, dispatch, trip counting, script-name registry, OOM/deferred rebuild, script loading. |
| Router | `NetRouter.cs` + `NetTransport.cs` | Transport-agnostic envelope/queue/rate-limit/authority core; `INetTransport` is the §8 seam (`LoopbackTransport` today). |
| Wire codec | `NetSerializer.cs` | Argument tags, table array/pair sections, all budgets, stack-based decode, wrapper disposal. |
| Script runtime | `ScriptRuntime.cs` | Script discovery, per-script envs, deterministic handle order, lot-root-at-load, frame `Tick`, teardown. |
| Lifecycle | `LuaManager.cs`, `BuilderScene.cs` | VM lifecycle/scratch path (`RunString`) and the lot world: handle registry (root = 0), load-order handles, script load via `StartScriptRuntime` (restartable — the §3.4 Test-mode re-entry seam), `_Process` tick, `StopScriptRuntime` in `_ExitTree` teardown order. |
| Verification | `NetSelfTest.cs` + `NetIntegrationTest.cs` | 169 unit checks (private Lua states only) + an opt-in 8-check heavy set (`OPENLOT_HEAVY_TESTS`) + 59 integration checks driving the REAL LuaManager/ScriptRuntime and live lot; all run headless under `#if DEBUG`. The fast set prints its wall-clock; integration waits a few physics frames, prints a "suite complete: N checks" line and exits non-zero if it aborts. |

**Invariants any future change must keep**
- Lua reaches C# only through `LotLuaApi`; `NetInvoke`/`NetRegister`/`CallServer`/`CallClient` are the only net entry points, and the raw table-taking forms stay out of creator reach.
- All net traffic is queued and dispatched from `NetRouter.Flush()`; nothing executes synchronously, and recursion must never become stack depth.
- Contract changes to bound methods, tag bytes, table sections or creator-visible names are breaking changes to creator-facing API — call them out, never slip them in.
- The watchdog hook is installed from Lua (error() is safe there) and cleared from C# after every unit; never throw managed exceptions through native Lua frames.

**Known limitations (see the design doc §6 for detail)**
- Scripts run at lot load only — hot re-attaching a script waits for §3.4 Test mode.
- Editor-spawned objects get handles only once a registration path touches them (option A covers everything present at load).
- `coroutine` is unavailable in scripts; single blocking built-ins cannot be preempted.
- Numbers cross as doubles (2^53 precision); an OOM rebuild loses script globals.
- **Native `lua54` is not packaged yet** — Lua runs only with a compatible `liblua54.so` on the library path; bundling it is required before Test mode ships.
- Load-time spawns are owned by their script and removed before a reload (F2 closed); handler spawns are unowned by design. Physics smoke-test timing sensitivity (F4, deferred). Scripting suspension is terminal until the lot reloads and shows as a red banner in the Viewport window.
- Origination rule: autonomous calls (lot load, start(), timers) are System in ALL modes; Player only inside an explicit interaction scope (`NetRouter.BeginPlayerInteraction`; Test mode: `SimulatePlayerInteraction`).

---

## Roadmap Execution Stages

The four phases below are the **execution order** and supersede the numeric section order: **Phase 1 is §4**,
which is hoisted in this document to sit right after §3.4 (see the banner and full section there). Phases 1–2 are
the MLP-critical path; Phase 3 (networking/headless) and Phase 4 (avatars/animation/audio) follow it, and the
per-milestone tags still govern what actually ships at MLP.

### Phase 1 — Core Storage & Pipeline (§4.1 / §4.2 — `.lot` Archive & `LotVFS`)
Everything durable depends on this, so it goes first: the single-file Zip `.lot` bundle (`manifest.json` /
`lot.json` / `scripts/` / `assets/`), the `LotVFS` streaming read path, versioning + migration, and autosave /
crash recovery to `user://autosave.lot`. Landing it here also **unblocks §2.5 (environment persistence, through
`lot.json`)**, **§2.4 (autosave, which lives inside §4.1)**, and hands §6.2 and §14.6 their embedded-asset read
path.

### Phase 2 — In-Session Input & Script Hooks (§3.7 / §5.2 / §5.3 — Attributed Input, Events, In-Lot Command UI & Lot UI Activation)
With a real lot now loadable, the next blocker is the missing in-session path from a player action to a script
handler: the in-lot command UI (§5.2), lot-UI activation / click routing and the new element kinds (§5.3), and
the `touched`/`clicked` event system carrying the §3.4 `BeginPlayerInteraction` attribution for input-driven
events — `clicked` and lot-UI presses (§3.7; the physics-side `touched` attribution lands earlier, with §3.7
itself). The input-driven half of all three shares one in-session blocker and lands together; event/timer
subscriptions ride in the archive's
`scripts/` (§4.1).

### Phase 3 — Networking & Headless Host (§8.1 / §8.2 — Headless `.lot` Execution, Session Model & the `INetTransport` seam)
Boot a `.lot` with no client (`--lot <path>`, no UI or rendering viewport nodes), stand up the real transport
behind `INetTransport` in place of `LoopbackTransport`, and land the host/join/list session model plus state
sync. `playerJoined`/`playerLeft` (§3.7), private P2P chat (§8.3) and the §3.12 per-player data ownership model
unblock here.

### Phase 4 — Avatars, Animations & Audio (§6.1 / §6.2 / §7.1 / §7.2 — Rigged Characters & GLTF, `.lanim` Tooling, 3D Audio)
The visible/audible layer, once Phase 1 can ship the assets and Phases 2–3 can drive and replicate them:
R6-style rigs + `.avlot` (§6.1), runtime GLTF/texture loading from the archive's `assets/` (§6.2), the `.lanim`
Animator tooling (§7.1), and sound playback / 3D positional audio / the audio pool bound into Lua (§7.2).

### Carve-outs and later work
- **Carve-outs inside the MLP set:** private P2P chat (8.3) and `playerJoined`/`playerLeft` (3.7) wait on §8.1's transport (Phase 3); environment persistence (2.5) and the player-data slot (3.12) are unblocked by §4.1's `.lot` archive (Phase 1); constraints/joints (3.6) shipped with the dynamic-body layer they needed, so forces/velocity and material properties (3.11) can now build on that same layer. Build the rest, land these as the dependency arrives.
- **MLP/Alpha scope (target Mid-late Nov 2026):** §1 Migration/terminal, §2 Creation Environment (Build Mode: 2.3 Inspector properties, 2.4 editor workflow, 2.5 lighting/environment, 2.7 precise scale gizmo, 2.8 output window), §3 Scripting/Runtime (§3.4 Test/Game mode + capsule character, §3.5 collision groups, §3.6 constraints/joints, §3.7 events/signals, §3.8 tween/timing, §3.9 input, §3.10 camera, §3.11 physics, §3.12 per-player data), §5 Terminal/Menu (§5.3 lot UI customization, §5.4 client settings, §5.5 keybindings, §5.6 tiling window modes), §8.3 chatting (bubbles/UI only), §4 Save/load (**Phase 1 — first**), §9 Testing loop (§9.3 in-client reporting). This is the playable, buildable core — no monetization, no providers, no plugins yet.
- **Post-MLP — Official Plugins (§13.4):** Ko-fi/Patreon first (cheapest, no custody issue), Roblox compatibility research started early even though shipping is later (it's the linchpin of the developer-migration growth thesis), Bender, the EZLot UI shell, and others after.
- **Post-MLP — Other Features:** §6.1 rigged characters, §7.1 animation system + §7.2 audio, §8.1–8.2 full networking, §10 mobile support (Android), §11 full plugin tiers, §12 benchmarks, §13.1–13.3 provider system and monetization, §14.1 controller support, §14.2 template lots, §14.3 localization (to be decided), §14.6 asset browser, §14.7 lot snapshots, §14.8 spawn points/teams/checkpoints, §14.9 leaderboard, §14.10 creator credits/license metadata, §14.11 accessibility, §15 first-launch setup.
- **Ongoing:** Polish pass driven by real MLP usage data rather than pre-guessed priorities.
